<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bappa Rawal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">8th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bappa Rawal is the legendary founder of the power of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guhila-dynasty">Guhila dynasty</a> in Mewar, said in dynastic tradition to have taken the fort of Chittor from its Mori rulers in the early eighth century. Later inscriptions and bardic accounts made him a devotee of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ekalinga">Ekalinga</a> and a hero of resistance to Arab raids, but his historical identity is uncertain, and scholars still debate which ruler named in inscriptions he may have been.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bappa-rawal.jpg" alt="Bappa Rawal" width="320" height="391" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Historicboyz · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bappa_Rawal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The story of Bappa's youth comes from the 15th-century Ekalinga Mahatmya, also called the Ekalinga Purana, and other bardic chronicles. They make him the ninth descendant of Guhadatta, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guhila-dynasty">Guhila dynasty</a>, and say that his father Nagaditya and all the other men of his family were killed fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhils">Bhils</a> of Idar. Bappa survived in disguise with two loyal Bhil attendants and was raised by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> woman of Nagda, who set him to herd cows.</p><p>According to the same tradition he met the sage Harit Rashi, who initiated him into the Shaivite order and told him to build the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ekalinga">Eklingji</a> temple at Nagda. After completing his spiritual training Bappa defeated his father's killers and founded the Mewar Kingdom, which the Ekalinga Mahatmya dates to 728 CE. In this account the Guhila rulers governed not as absolute sovereigns but as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwan">Diwans</a>, custodians ruling on behalf of the family deity, Lord Eklingnath ji.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Some records say that Bappa captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chittor-fort">Chittor Fort</a>, the ancient Chitrakuta, from the mlechchhas. R. C. Majumdar held that the Moris ruled Chittor when Arab forces invaded north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> around 725 CE, that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> defeated them, and that a confederacy including Bappa then defeated the Arabs; his fame in this war, Majumdar thought, led to his being wrongly remembered as the dynasty's founder. R. V. Somani placed him in the anti-Arab confederacy of the Gurjara-Pratihara ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagabhata-i">Nagabhata I</a>, while Shyam Manohar Mishra suggested he began as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the Mori ruler Manuraja and later replaced him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Because Bappa means father and Rawal is a royal title, scholars such as C. V. Vaidya, D. R. Bhandarkar, G. H. Ojha and Kaviraj Shyamaldas regard the name as an epithet rather than a proper name. He appears in some genealogical inscriptions, such as the Unawas inscription of 959 CE, but not in others, such as the Atpur inscription of 977 CE. Ojha and others identified him with Kalabhoja; Dasharatha Sharma and D. C. Sircar with Shiladitya; Bhandarkar with Kalabhoja's son Khumana.</p><p>Gold coins bearing the legend Shri Voppa or Shri Vopparaja, with a trident, a linga and a bull, have been attributed to him by Ojha and others, though a 1960 journal of the Numismatic Society of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> assigned them instead to King Vappuka of the Surasena dynasty. A temple to Bappa Rawal stands at Mathatha, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ekalinga">Eklingji</a> temple, 24 kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udaipur">Udaipur</a>, and the 1925 silent film Mewadpati Bappa Rawal told his life.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bappa Rawal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Dynastic tradition makes <b>Bappa Rawal</b>, who is said to have taken the great fort of Chittor from its Mori rulers in the early eighth century, the true founder, and the register’s date of 728 follows this tradition; whether Bappa is the same person as a ruler named in…</blockquote><a href="/empire/udaipur-state">Udaipur State · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Eklingji, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/nepal">Nepal</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=24.75,73.72&amp;year=764" aria-label="Eklingji on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Bappa Rawal"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6056 5385h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6056 5385h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=24.75,73.72&amp;year=764">What was here in 764 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
